\A{faq} KiTTY++ \i{FAQ}

This appendix answers common questions about KiTTY++ and its tools.
To report a problem or request a feature, see \k{feedback}.

\H{faq-support} Features supported in KiTTY++

\I{supported features}To find out whether KiTTY++ supports a
particular feature, read this manual, in particular the chapter on
KiTTY++'s own configuration options (\k{kitty-config}). The file
\c{FEATURES.md} in the KiTTY++ repository at
\W{https://github.com/hknet/KiTTY}\cw{https://github.com/hknet/KiTTY}
lists every feature KiTTY++ adds to PuTTY.

\S{faq-disksettings}{Question} Does KiTTY++ support storing its
settings in a disk file?

Yes. With \c{savemode=dir} in \c{kitty.ini} (the default of the
portable \c{kitty_portable.exe}) KiTTY++ keeps its saved sessions, SSH
host keys and other state in a folder store instead of in the
Registry. By default the folder store lives beside the program. See
\k{config-file}.

\S{faq-password-remember}{Question} Can KiTTY++ \i{remember my
password} so I don't have to type it every time?

Yes. A password stored on a session's Login panel is used to log in.
KiTTY++ protects every stored password: in the Registry it is
encrypted for your Windows account, in a folder store by the master
password, or for your Windows account if
\c{PortablePasswordProtection=dpapi} is set. A password typed at the login prompt can also end up stored
with the session (see \k{kitty-cfg-passwords}).

Anyone who gains access to your Windows account can still use a
stored password. For SSH, public key authentication is more flexible
and more secure. See \k{pubkey} for a full discussion of public key
authentication.

\S{faq-hostkeys}{Question} Is there an option to turn off the
\I{verifying the host key}annoying host key prompts?

No, there isn't.

Those host key prompts are the \e{whole point} of SSH. Without them,
all the cryptographic technology SSH uses to secure your session does
nothing more than make an attacker's job slightly harder: instead of
sitting between you and the server with a packet sniffer, the attacker
must actually subvert a router and start modifying the packets going
back and forth. That is not much harder than sniffing. Without host
key checking, it goes completely undetected by client or server.

Host key checking is your guarantee that the encryption you put on
your data at the client end is the \e{same} encryption taken off the
data at the server end. It guarantees that nobody removed and replaced
it somewhere on the way. Instead of subverting a router, the attacker
must now perform a brute-force attack against at least one
military-strength cipher.

If host key checking gets in the way of a specific job \dash perhaps
the interactive host key prompt hangs an automated batch job that
uses kscp or klink \dash then the right fix is to store the correct
host key in advance, or to use the \cw{\-hostkey} command-line option
(see \k{using-cmdline-hostkey}). That way the right key is accepted
and the wrong ones are not.

If you manage and connect to a lot of servers with different host
keys, consider a \q{certification authority}, so that you configure
KiTTY++ once on each client machine to accept host keys from any of
your servers in future. See \k{config-ssh-kex-cert}.

\H{faq-details} Details of KiTTY++'s operation

\S{faq-term}{Question} What \i{terminal type} does KiTTY++ use?

By default, KiTTY++ announces its terminal type to SSH (and Telnet)
servers as \c{xterm-256color}. It behaves close enough to \c{xterm}
that this works well. A session saved by an older version keeps the
\c{xterm} it was saved with. See \k{config-termtype}.

KiTTY++ also supports some terminal \i{control sequences} not supported
by the real \cw{xterm}: notably the Linux console sequences that
reconfigure the colour palette, as well as the title bar control sequences
used by \i\cw{DECterm} (which are different from the \cw{xterm} ones).
KiTTY++ supports both kinds of title sequence.

\S{faq-settings}{Question} Where does KiTTY++ store its data?

On Windows, KiTTY++ stores most of its data (saved sessions, SSH host
keys) in the \i{Registry}. The precise location is

\c HKEY_CURRENT_USER\Software\kapper.net\KiTTY

and within that area, saved sessions are stored under \c{Sessions}
while host keys are stored under \c{SshHostKeys}. (Settings left
behind by earlier KiTTY versions under \c{Software\\9bis.com\\KiTTY}
or by PuTTY under \c{Software\\SimonTatham\\PuTTY} are read as a
fallback, so they keep working.) With \c{savemode=dir} all of this
lives in a folder store instead - see \k{config-file}.

KiTTY++ also requires a random number seed file, to improve the
unpredictability of randomly chosen data needed as part of the SSH
cryptography. This is stored by default in a file called \i\c{PUTTY.RND}
in the \q{Application Data} directory, or failing that, in one of a
number of fallback locations. If you
want to change the location of the random number seed file, you can
put your chosen pathname in the Registry, at

\c HKEY_CURRENT_USER\Software\kapper.net\KiTTY\RandSeedFile

To delete all this data, see \k{faq-cleanup}.

\S{faq-trust-sigils} Why do small \i{KiTTY++ icon}s appear next to the login
prompts?

Some lines of text in the terminal window are marked with a small copy
of the KiTTY++ icon (as far as pixels allow).

This shows trustworthiness. When the icon appears next to a line of
text, KiTTY++ itself generated that line. The server did not send it.

Text that comes from the server does not have this icon. The server
cannot fake it. No control sequence from the server makes
KiTTY++ draw its own icon. If the server moves the cursor back up to a
line that \e{already} has an icon and overwrites the text, the icon
disappears.

This way you can distinguish a legitimate prompt, in which KiTTY++ itself
prompts you for your private key passphrase, from a fake prompt, in
which the server sends the identical text to trick you into giving
\e{it} your private key passphrase.

\S{faq-plink-pause} Why does klink show \q{Press Return to
begin session}?

If you use klink for an interactive SSH session, then after the login
phase has finished, it presents a final interactive prompt reading
\q{Access granted. Press Return to begin session}.

This is another defence against servers trying to mimic the real
authentication prompts after the session has started. When you pass
through that prompt, you know that everything after it comes from the
server and not from klink itself, so any request for your private key
passphrase should be treated with suspicion.

klink cannot use the defence described in \k{faq-trust-sigils}: klink
runs \e{in} the terminal, so anything it can write into the terminal,
the server could write in the same way after the session starts. A
separator line without a pause would not help either, because the
server could simply move the cursor back up to it and overwrite it
(probably with a brief flicker, but you might easily miss that). The
only robust defence is this pause.

If you trust your server not to be abusive, you can turn this off. It
also does not appear in various other circumstances where klink can be
confident it isn't necessary. See \k{plink-option-antispoof} for
details.

\H{faq-howto} HOWTO questions

\S{faq-login}{Question} What login name / password should I use?

KiTTY++ is a communications tool for making connections to other
computers. The KiTTY++ developers do not administer the computers you
connect to, so they cannot help with this.

If you know the name of the computer you want to connect to, but not
the login name or password to use, contact whoever administers that
computer. If you don't know who that is, see the next question for
some possible ways to find out.

\S{faq-commands}{Question} \I{commands on the server}What commands
can I type into my KiTTY++ terminal window?

Read the manuals of \e{the computer you have connected to}, or contact
its administrator.

KiTTY++ does not process the commands you type into it. It makes a
connection to another computer. It passes the commands you type to that
computer and passes that computer's responses back to you. The range
of commands you can use therefore depends on what kind of computer you
have connected to and what software runs on it, not on KiTTY++.

To find the administrator of your server, remember how you found out
the host name in the KiTTY++ configuration. If somebody sent you that
host name by e-mail, for example, contact the person who sent it. If
your company's IT department provided ready-made KiTTY++ saved
sessions, then that IT department can probably also help with the
commands you can type during those sessions.

\S{faq-startmax}{Question} How can I make KiTTY++ start up \i{maximise}d?

Turn on \q{Maximize on startup} in the session's settings (see
\k{kitty-cfg-behaviour}). Alternatively, create a Windows shortcut to
start KiTTY++ from and set it to \q{Run Maximized}.

\S{faq-startsess}{Question} How can I create a \i{Windows shortcut} to
start a particular saved session directly?

To run a KiTTY++ session saved under the name \q{\cw{mysession}},
create a Windows shortcut that invokes KiTTY++ with a command line
like

\c \path\name\to\kitty.exe -load "mysession"

See \k{using-cmdline-load}.

\S{faq-startssh}{Question} How can I start an SSH session straight
from the command line?

Use the command line \c{kitty \-ssh host.name}. Alternatively, create
a saved session that specifies the SSH protocol. Then start the saved
session as shown in \k{faq-startsess}.

\S{faq-ipc}{Question} How can I use KiTTY++ to make an SSH connection
from within another program?

Use klink, the command-line connection tool. If you can start klink as
a second Windows process and arrange for your primary process to send
data to the klink process and receive data from it through pipes, then
you can make SSH connections from your program.

\S{faq-cutpaste}{Question} How do I \i{copy and paste} between KiTTY++ and
other Windows applications?

By default, copy and paste works similarly to the X Window System. You
use the left mouse button to select text in the KiTTY++ window. The act
of selection \e{automatically} copies the text to the clipboard: there
is no need to press Ctrl-Ins or Ctrl-C or anything else. In fact,
pressing Ctrl-C sends a Ctrl-C character to the other end of your
connection (just like it does the rest of the time), which may have
unpleasant effects. The \e{only} thing you need to do, to copy text to
the clipboard, is to select it.

If you prefer a more \q{Windows native} interface for copy and paste,
via keystrokes or menu actions, with nothing happening automatically
on select, then you can reconfigure the behaviour flexibly in KiTTY++'s
configuration. See \k{config-selection} for details.

To paste the clipboard contents into a KiTTY++ window, by default you
click the right mouse button. If you have a three-button mouse and
are used to X applications, you can configure pasting to be done by
the middle button instead, but this is not the default because most
Windows users don't have a middle button at all.

You can also paste by pressing Shift-Ins.

\S{faq-options}{Question} How do I use all KiTTY++'s features (public
keys, proxying, cipher selection, etc.) in kscp, ksftp and klink?

Most major features (e.g., public keys, port forwarding) are available
through command line options. See \k{using-general-opts}.

Not all features are accessible from the command line. To use them,
create a KiTTY++ saved session, then use the name of the saved session
on the command line in place of a hostname. This works for kscp, ksftp
and klink (but don't expect port forwarding in the file transfer
applications!).

\S{faq-pscp}{Question} How do I use kscp.exe? When I double-click it
I get a command prompt window which then closes instantly.

kscp is a command-line application, not a GUI application. If you
run it without arguments, it prints a help message and terminates.

To use kscp properly, run it from a Command Prompt window. See
\k{pscp} in the documentation for more details.

\S{faq-pscp-spaces}{Question} \I{spaces in filenames}How do I use
kscp to copy a file whose name has spaces in?

If kscp uses the newer SFTP protocol (which is usual with most modern
servers), this is straightforward. Filenames with spaces in are
specified using a single pair of quotes in the obvious way:

\c kscp "local file" user@host:
\c kscp user@host:"remote file" .

However, if kscp uses the older SCP protocol for some reason, things
are more confusing. If you specify a file at the local end, you use
one set of quotes as you would normally do:

\c kscp "local filename with spaces" user@host:
\c kscp user@host:myfile "local filename with spaces"

But if the filename you specify is on the \e{remote} side, you
have to use backslashes and two sets of quotes:

\c kscp user@host:"\"remote filename with spaces\"" local_filename
\c kscp local_filename user@host:"\"remote filename with spaces\""

Worse still, in a remote-to-local copy you have to specify the local
file name explicitly, otherwise kscp complains that they don't
match (unless you specified the \c{\-unsafe} option). The following
command gives an error message:

\c c:\>kscp user@host:"\"oo er\"" .
\c warning: remote host tried to write to a file called 'oo er'
\c          when we requested a file called '"oo er"'.

Instead, you need to specify the local file name in full:

\c c:\>kscp user@host:"\"oo er\"" "oo er"

\H{faq-trouble} Troubleshooting

\S{faq-colours}{Question} I clicked on a colour in the \ii{Colours}
panel but the colour didn't change in my terminal.

That isn't how the Colours panel works.

During the course of a session, KiTTY++ potentially uses \e{all} the
colours listed in the Colours panel. It does not use only the one you
choose. The purpose of the Colours panel is to let you adjust the
appearance of all the colours. So to change the colour of the
cursor, for example, you would select \q{Cursor Colour}, press the
\q{Modify} button, then select a new colour from the dialog box that
appeared. Similarly, if you want your session to appear in green,
you should select \q{Default Foreground} and press \q{Modify}.
Clicking on \q{ANSI Green} won't turn your session green. It only
lets you adjust the \e{shade} of green used when the server instructs
KiTTY++ to display green text.

\S{faq-bce}{Question} When I run full-colour applications, I see
areas of black space where colour ought to be, or vice versa.

You almost certainly need to change the \q{Use \i{background colour} to
erase screen} setting in the Terminal panel. If there is too much
black space (the commoner situation), you should enable it, while if
there is too much colour, you should disable it. (See \k{config-erase}.)

\S{faq-idleout}{Question} My KiTTY++ sessions unexpectedly close after
they are \I{idle connections}idle for a while.

Some types of \i{firewall} forget about a connection through them if
the connection does nothing for too long. So does almost any router
doing Network Address Translation (\i{NAT}, also known as IP
masquerading). This causes the connection to be rudely cut off when
contact is resumed.

You can try to combat this by configuring KiTTY++ to send
\e{keepalives}: packets of data which have no effect on the actual
session, but which reassure the router or firewall that the network
connection is still active and worth remembering about.

Keepalives don't solve everything, unfortunately. They give greater
robustness against this sort of router, but they can also cause a
\e{loss} of robustness against network dropouts. See
\k{config-keepalive} in the documentation for more discussion of
this.

\S{faq-puttyputty}{Question} When I \cw{cat} a binary file, text
I didn't type appears on my command line.

Don't do that, then.

When KiTTY++ receives the character Control-E from the remote server,
it sends back the configured \i{answerback} string as if that string
had been entered at the keyboard. By default that string is empty, so
nothing is sent. If you set one, writing a binary file to your
terminal is likely to output many Control-E characters. Your next
command line then holds the answerback string several times over.

To stop this, clear the answerback string (see
\k{config-answerback}). Writing binary files to your terminal is
likely to cause various other unpleasant behaviour too.

\S{faq-wintitle}{Question} When I \cw{cat} a binary file, my \i{window
title} changes to a nonsense string.

Don't do that, then.

It is designed behaviour that KiTTY++ can adjust the window title on
instructions from the server. Normally the control sequence that does
this is only sent deliberately, by programs that know what they are
doing and intend to put meaningful text in the window title. Writing a
binary file to your terminal runs the risk of sending the same control
sequence by accident, which causes unexpected changes in the window
title. Don't do it.

\S{faq-keyboard}{Question} One or more \I{keyboard}\i{function keys}
don't do what I expected in a server-side application.

KiTTY++ responds to function key presses by sending a sequence of
control characters to the server. If a function key isn't doing what
you expect, it is likely that the character sequence your application
expects to receive is not the same as the one KiTTY++ sends. First try
the relevant options in the KiTTY++ Keyboard panel.

If none of them helps, find out \e{what} sequence the application
expects. The simplest way is to find some other terminal environment
in which that function key \e{does} work. Then investigate what
sequence the function key sends there. One reasonably easy way to do
this on a \i{Unix} system is to type the command \i\c{cat}, then
press the function key. This is likely to produce output of the form
\c{^[[11~}. You can also do this in KiTTY++, to find out what sequence
the function key produces there.

Then report it as described in \k{feedback}, in the form \q{I wanted
the F1 key to send \c{^[[11~}, but instead it's sending \c{^[OP}, can
this be done?}, or something similar. Just the name of the
application, the server operating system and the key that doesn't work
is not enough to reproduce the problem.

\S{faq-privkey-control-moved}{Question} I can't find where
to configure my SSH private key.

The \q{\ii{Private key} file for authentication} control, where you
specify a \c{.\i{PPK}} file for SSH public key authentication, is on
the \q{Credentials} panel of the configuration dialog. You can find it
by opening the \q{SSH} category in the tree view on the left, then
opening the \q{Auth} subcategory under that, then clicking on
\q{Credentials}. On this page you'll find the \q{Browse...} button you
need to select a \c{.PPK} file for authentication, as described in
\k{config-ssh-privkey}.

(PuTTY versions before 0.78 had this control on the \q{Auth} panel, so
many online how-to guides still describe it there.)

\H{faq-secure} Security questions

\S{faq-publicpc}{Question} Is it safe for me to download KiTTY++ and
use it on a public PC?

It depends on whether you trust that PC. If you don't trust the
public PC, don't use KiTTY++ on it. Don't use any other software you
plan to type passwords into either. It might be watching your
keystrokes, or it might tamper with the KiTTY++ binary you download.
There is \e{no} program safe enough that you can run it on an
actively malicious PC and get away with typing passwords into it.

If you do trust the PC, then it's probably OK to use KiTTY++ on it
(but if you don't trust the network, then the KiTTY++ download might
be tampered with, so it would be better to carry KiTTY++ with you on a
USB stick).

\S{faq-cleanup}{Question} What does KiTTY++ leave on a system? How can
I \i{clean up} after it?

KiTTY++ leaves some Registry entries and a random seed file on
the PC (see \k{faq-settings}). Windows 7 and up also remember some
information about recently launched sessions for the \q{jump list}
feature.

If you use KiTTY++ on a public PC, or somebody else's PC, you might
want to clean this information up when you leave. To do that, run the
command \c{kitty \-cleanup}. See \k{using-cleanup} in the documentation
for more detail. (Note that this only removes settings for the
currently logged-in user on \i{multi-user systems}.)

A portable copy keeps its sessions and other state in files beside the
program instead (see \k{config-file}).
